course: dictionary
lesson: 3025
title: Eager and keen
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Eager» means wanting very much to do or have something, and feeling keen and excited about it. Context gives you the clues. «The eager pupil put up her hand before the teacher had finished the question.» She is keen to answer, so eager must mean keen.
explain: Eager can also describe faces and looks. «Eager faces» are bright and interested, as if the people cannot wait to see what happens next. Look at the words after eager, such as «eager to learn» or «eager to please». They tell you what the person wants to do.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in keen and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have got it. Here it does: «The eager pupil» can become «the keen pupil» with no change in meaning.
fact: Eager means wanting very much to do or have something; keen and excited.
fact: Eager is an adjective, so you can say more eager or most eager.
fact: «Eager beaver» is a playful name for someone who is very keen, like a pupil who always wants to learn.
fact: Keen means much the same as eager, as in «keen to learn».
fact: «Gung ho» came into English in the 1940s from a Chinese slogan, and now means very, and sometimes too, eager.
exercise: «The children had eager faces as the bell rang for the picnic.» What do the eager faces show? | Anger | *Keen interest and excitement | Sadness | Being very cold
exercise: Which word means keen and excited? | Heavy | Grumpy | Sleepy | *Eager
exercise: «The eager pupil put up her hand before the teacher had finished.» Find and copy one word that means the same as eager. | pupil | hand | *keen | teacher
test: «Grandad was eager for the football to start.» What does eager for mean here? | Sure the team would lose | Afraid of the game | Bored of waiting | *Wanting very much to see it begin
test: «Eager beaver» is a playful name for someone who is what? | Very tired | Very grumpy | Very quiet | *Very keen
exam: Which sentence uses eager correctly? | «The eager table fell over in the rain.» | «The eager window was cold and wet.» | *«The eager children counted down the days to the trip.» | «The eager sky went to sleep early.»
